   "parent_message_uuid": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000"
  },
  {
   "uuid": "d660101e-6301-46fd-a7c7-ec8d56450990",
   "text": "",
   "content": [
    {
     "start_timestamp": "2026-05-24T20:20:02.000000Z",
     "stop_timestamp": "2026-05-24T20:20:13.106000Z",
     "type": "text",
     "text": "Both are likely; check in this order:\n\n1. **Chain wear**: at ~3000 miles an 11-speed chain is often at or past 0.5% wear. A worn chain skips under load, especially on the cogs you use most (often the middle ones). Check with a chain checker (~$10). If it's at 0.75% or more, the cassette is probably worn too, and a new chain on an old cassette can skip *more*.\n2. **Cable tension**: shift to the smallest cog, then use the barrel adjuster. If it hesitates going up the cassette, add tension (turn the adjuster counterclockwise) a quarter turn at a time. If it hesitates going down, reduce.\n3. **Bent derailleur hanger**: if tuning helps some gears but makes others worse, the hanger may be bent (common after the bike falls on its right side).\n\nGiven skipping under load specifically in the most-used gears, my bet is chain + cassette wear.",
     "citations": []
    }
   ],
   "sender": "assistant",
